Composited from the real `LogPane.plain()` and `render_sidebar()`. The frame
itself is asserted by driving the actual `Application` over
`create_pipe_input()` - see TESTS.


HOW THE TURN'S OUTPUT GETS INTO THE PANE
----------------------------------------
Not by rewriting call sites. The REPL hands ONE `rich.Console` down through
every route, and rich exposes `Console.file` and `Console.width` as settable -
so the frame points that console at a `PaneWriter` for the length of the turn
and puts it back in `finally`. Panels, markdown, diffs and the turn renderer's
rows all land in the pane, and nothing had to learn that a frame exists.

`PaneWriter.isatty()` returns True so rich keeps emitting ANSI; `LogPane`
parses it back into styled fragments. Verified on a real `rich.Panel`.


THREE THINGS THE SPEC DID NOT MENTION, ALL OF WHICH BITE
--------------------------------------------------------
1. THE APPROVAL PROMPT. Two prompt_toolkit `Application`s cannot run at once,
   and the approval prompt is one - so inside a frame it cannot simply be
   called. `reading_input()` in `safety/approval.py` already fired an open
   hook; it now fires a matching CLOSE hook, and `LiveConsole._suspend_frame`
   uses the pair to hold `run_in_terminal` for exactly the question's lifetime:

       stand_down()  -> schedule run_in_terminal(waiter.wait, in_executor=True)
       [ the frame drops the alternate screen; the question is asked normally ]
       resume()      -> waiter.set(); the frame is restored

   `in_executor=True` so the event loop keeps turning while a human decides,
   and the wait is bounded at 900s - a handover that never came back would be
   a terminal the user cannot reach. `set_frame(None)` also releases, so a turn
   that ends mid-approval cannot park a waiter forever.

2. CTRL+C. In full-screen mode prompt_toolkit owns the keyboard and the SIGINT
   handler never fires. `_RequestRunner.interrupt()` is a new entry point so
   the key binding lands exactly where the signal did - graceful cancel, run
   marked CANCELLED, prompt comes back.

3. MOUSE CAPTURE COSTS TEXT SELECTION. With `mouse_support` on the terminal
   stops handing click-drag to its own selection. Most terminals still allow it
   with Shift held; not all do. Bound to F2, and the statusline says which
   state you are in - `mouse` / `MOUSE OFF`.


THE BUG THE TESTS FOUND, WHICH IS THE ONE THAT MATTERED
-------------------------------------------------------
The wheel did nothing.

`scroll()` applied its delta to whatever `offset` held. While following the
tail, `offset` was last set for the height of the PREVIOUS paint - and when the
window is taller than that, the old offset is already past this height's limit,
so a scroll up clamped straight back to the bottom and re-armed follow. Net
effect: the view never moved.

    limit = self.max_offset(height)
    base  = limit if self.follow else min(self.offset, limit)   # <- the fix
    self.offset = max(0, min(limit, base + rows))
    self.follow = self.offset >= limit

Caught by `test_paging_moves_about_a_screen` and
`test_the_wheel_scrolls_the_log_and_nothing_else`, both of which failed on the
first run for the same reason.


FOLLOW-TAIL
-----------
The detail that decides whether the scrollback is usable during a live turn.
New output must not yank you back to the bottom while you are reading something
forty rounds up. The pane follows the tail only while it is ALREADY at the
tail; scroll up once and it stays put until you scroll back to the end, which
re-arms it. Asserted directly:

    test_scrolling_up_stops_new_output_yanking_you_back
        scroll up, write ten more lines, assert the visible text is BYTE FOR
        BYTE what it was.

Eviction is handled in the same place: when a line falls off the top of a full
scrollback, the offset moves with it, so the content you are reading does not
drift up a row at a time (`test_eviction_does_not_shift_what_you_are_reading_
off_by_one`).


OPT-IN, AND THE FALLBACK
------------------------
`/tui`, `/tui on`, `/tui off`, or `SHAMSU_TUI=1`. Armed per session on purpose:
a full-screen mode that silently survived a restart would take the terminal on
a run where you wanted a plain log to copy out of.

Three display modes now, chosen once per turn in `_dispatch_turn`:

    framed TUI      /tui or SHAMSU_TUI=1
    pinned prompt   the default on a terminal that can host one
    plain stream    a pipe, a CI runner, a console with no screen buffer

The last is not a degraded path - it is exactly what every turn did before any
of this work.

Below 90 columns the sidebar is dropped: 30 columns of pane out of 70 leaves
the log unreadable.


WHAT I HAVE NOT PROVEN
----------------------
A real Windows terminal. This session has no TTY, so the frame is asserted
through `create_pipe_input()` + `DummyOutput()` / `PlainTextOutput` rather than
by looking at one. That covers construction, painting, key handling, mouse
events, input routing and teardown - a layout that cannot be built or a control
that raises while painting fails in the suite - but it does not cover how the
alternate screen behaves on your machine.


